1
00:01:03,363 --> 00:01:06,527
<i>Sing hallelujah praise the Lord</i>

2
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:10,467
<i>Sing with a cheerful voice</i>

3
00:01:10,637 --> 00:01:18,568
<i>Exalt our God with one
accord And in his name rejoice</i>

4
00:01:18,745 --> 00:01:22,739
<i>Ne'er cease to sing
O ransomed host</i>

5
00:01:22,916 --> 00:01:26,978
<i>Praise Father,
Son and Holy Ghost</i>

6
00:01:27,154 --> 00:01:31,057
<i>Until in realms of endless light</i>

7
00:01:31,224 --> 00:01:35,127
<i>Your praises shall unite</i>

8
00:01:35,295 --> 00:01:43,295
<i>Sing hallelujah praise the
Lord Sing with a cheerful voice</i>

9
00:01:43,537 --> 00:01:51,537
<i>Exalt our God with one
accord And in his name rejoice</i>

10
00:01:51,845 --> 00:01:55,748
<i>Ne'er cease to sing
O ransomed host</i>

11
00:01:55,916 --> 00:02:00,012
<i>Praise Father,
Son and Holy Ghost</i>

12
00:02:00,187 --> 00:02:07,594
<i>Until in realms of endless
light Your praises shall unite</i>

13
00:02:08,829 --> 00:02:16,794
<i>There we to all eternity
Shall join th'angelic lays</i>

14
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:24,969
<i>And sing in perfect harmony
To God our savior's praise</i>

15
00:02:25,145 --> 00:02:29,515
<i>He hath redeemed us by his blood</i>

16
00:02:29,683 --> 00:02:34,917
<i>And made us kings
And priests to God</i>

17
00:02:35,088 --> 00:02:41,824
<i>For us, for us,
the lamb was slain</i>

18
00:02:42,829 --> 00:02:50,829
<i>Praise ye the Lord Amen</i>

19
00:02:51,705 --> 00:02:55,506
Ah. Eat your heart out,
Mormon Tabernacle.

20
00:02:56,877 --> 00:02:59,278
That was terrific. And, Cliff,
not to beat a dead horse,

21
00:02:59,446 --> 00:03:01,766
but if you're having trouble
hitting that F above middle C,

22
00:03:02,182 --> 00:03:04,242
don't feel the
need to belt it out.

23
00:03:04,418 --> 00:03:07,582
Sorry. I'm sorry. I didn't
realize I did that. Sorry.

24
00:03:07,754 --> 00:03:09,746
Now, people, before
we, uh, call it a day,

25
00:03:09,923 --> 00:03:11,283
I have a few
announcements to make.

26
00:03:11,425 --> 00:03:14,884
First all, I'm going to be,
uh, away for six weeks.

27
00:03:15,062 --> 00:03:16,223
Aw.

28
00:03:16,396 --> 00:03:19,127
Listen, people, I love you,
but I love my wife more,

29
00:03:19,299 --> 00:03:22,064
and if you'd had to be put up
with my snoring and my humming

30
00:03:22,235 --> 00:03:24,136
and atrocious eating
habits for 20 years,

31
00:03:24,304 --> 00:03:26,603
you'd deserve a trip
to, uh, Europe as well.

32
00:03:28,275 --> 00:03:31,211
Now, the good news is that
you'll be in the capable hands

33
00:03:31,378 --> 00:03:34,314
of our assistant choir
director, Ben Matlock.

34
00:03:40,954 --> 00:03:42,718
Now, also on the
subject of departures,

35
00:03:42,889 --> 00:03:44,084
our own mezzo-soprano,

36
00:03:44,257 --> 00:03:46,488
Professor Ruth Emerson,
is about to leave us

37
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:50,927
to accept her new responsibilities
as a full professor of psychology

38
00:03:51,098 --> 00:03:52,726
at Wellington University.

39
00:03:52,899 --> 00:03:54,527
Congratulations.

40
00:03:56,369 --> 00:03:57,928
Mickey.

41
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:12,951
It's your favorite.
German chocolate cake

42
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:16,682
- with hazelnut frosting.
- Oh, you're the sweetest, Mickey.

43
00:04:16,857 --> 00:04:20,726
Now, listen, I don't wanna hear one
word about calories or cholesterol.

44
00:04:20,894 --> 00:04:24,228
I bought the cake. The
least you guys can do is eat it.

45
00:04:26,299 --> 00:04:29,235
- Congratulations, Benjamin.
- Thanks, Wally.

46
00:04:29,402 --> 00:04:30,836
- Have a little cake?
- I think not.

47
00:04:31,004 --> 00:04:33,684
The museum just got in a bunch of
antique books they need appraised.

48
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:35,331
- You sound excited.
McDANlELS: I am.

49
00:04:35,509 --> 00:04:39,139
Rumor has it they have a first
edition of Henry Ward Beecher's

50
00:04:39,312 --> 00:04:41,474
<i>Proverbs From Plymouth Pulpit.</i>

51
00:04:41,648 --> 00:04:44,083
- Valuable.
- Oh-ho. I'll see you next time.

52
00:04:44,251 --> 00:04:46,846
- Okay.
- Bye-bye.

53
00:04:47,754 --> 00:04:49,450
Boy, that guy
really likes books.

54
00:04:49,623 --> 00:04:53,287
Well, I say, head of the English
Department at Baxter University.

55
00:04:53,460 --> 00:04:55,622
- Want a piece of cake?
- You got a small piece here.

56
00:04:55,796 --> 00:04:57,458
Yeah.

57
00:04:57,631 --> 00:04:59,429
Yeah.

58
00:05:00,333 --> 00:05:03,895
- You have a second, Mr. Matlock?
- Oh, sure. Sure. Come on.

59
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:05,402
You know, I love to sing.

60
00:05:05,572 --> 00:05:09,873
Ahem, well, I know, I see you singing
every time you come into chapel.

61
00:05:10,043 --> 00:05:11,306
I know I'm just a janitor,

62
00:05:11,912 --> 00:05:14,973
but I was wondering, now that
you're the new choir director,

63
00:05:15,148 --> 00:05:17,276
whether there might be
a spot for me in the choir.

64
00:05:17,450 --> 00:05:19,282
Oh, well, Mickey...

65
00:05:19,452 --> 00:05:21,648
I know all the songs.
I'm a great tenor.

66
00:05:21,822 --> 00:05:24,155
Well, I know. I
know. I know that.

67
00:05:24,324 --> 00:05:30,696
That's why Ron said no to start with is,
ahem, we've got way too many tenors.

68
00:05:30,864 --> 00:05:34,130
- I'll sing bass.
- Mickey...

69
00:05:34,301 --> 00:05:36,668
Well, I don't know why he's in.

70
00:05:36,837 --> 00:05:39,136
I'm a much better
tenor than he is.

71
00:05:39,873 --> 00:05:43,332
I can hit an F above middle
C while I'm drinking water.

72
00:05:43,510 --> 00:05:45,172
Hey!

73
00:05:58,024 --> 00:06:00,064
McDANlELS: Some of these
are fabulous, Ms. Reynolds.

74
00:06:00,193 --> 00:06:01,889
Are they? I'm embarrassed.

75
00:06:02,062 --> 00:06:04,054
As I told you, they
were left to the museum.

76
00:06:04,231 --> 00:06:08,225
I'm completely unfamiliar with
antique books and their value.

77
00:06:08,401 --> 00:06:09,699
You want all of these appraised?

78
00:06:09,870 --> 00:06:13,272
- Would that be too much trouble?
- Oh, no, shouldn't take too long.

79
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:15,160
I'll come to your office
when I'm done here.

80
00:06:15,308 --> 00:06:17,334
Oh, please, take your
time, Mr. McDaniels.

81
00:06:17,510 --> 00:06:19,741
We haven't even decided
what to do with these books

82
00:06:19,913 --> 00:06:21,753
after we inventory them
and have them restored.

83
00:06:22,549 --> 00:06:24,950
I'll see you in a
couple of hours.

84
00:08:48,995 --> 00:08:50,964
A Himson Trilogy.

85
00:08:51,131 --> 00:08:53,430
This is remarkable,
Mr. McDaniels.

86
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:56,627
I believe there are only three
complete sets of Himson's work

87
00:08:56,803 --> 00:09:00,171
- in the entire country right now.
- Unbelievable.

88
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:03,208
And you want to just
donate them to the library.

89
00:09:03,810 --> 00:09:07,406
What better place for literary
treasures like these than a library,

90
00:09:07,580 --> 00:09:09,674
where the public can
appreciate their beauty?

91
00:09:09,849 --> 00:09:13,149
- They must be worth a fortune.
- Thousands.

92
00:09:13,319 --> 00:09:15,720
I hope there's some tax
benefit you could derive.

93
00:09:15,889 --> 00:09:19,951
Mr. Harrington, I
don't want a tax benefit.

94
00:09:20,126 --> 00:09:21,924
This isn't about money.

95
00:09:22,729 --> 00:09:26,325
Mr. McDaniels, I really
don't know what to say.

96
00:09:29,436 --> 00:09:31,769
Oh, there is one thing.

97
00:09:32,405 --> 00:09:34,806
Make the sure books
never leave the library.

98
00:09:34,974 --> 00:09:37,000
I'd hate to think that
somebody might steal them.

99
00:09:37,177 --> 00:09:39,442
You have my word on that.

100
00:10:02,769 --> 00:10:04,795
McDANlELS: There are
50 multiple choice questions.

101
00:10:04,971 --> 00:10:08,271
You will have one hour
to complete the exam.

102
00:10:08,441 --> 00:10:09,534
I have to run an errand,

103
00:10:09,709 --> 00:10:12,440
but Professor Petri will
proctor in my absence.

104
00:10:12,612 --> 00:10:15,980
If you have any questions,
feel free to ask him.

105
00:10:16,149 --> 00:10:17,447
Begin.

106
00:10:22,689 --> 00:10:25,625
Thanks, Tom, for filling
in on such short notice.

107
00:10:25,792 --> 00:10:29,024
Joan Reynolds over at the museum
got in some more books yesterday.

108
00:10:29,195 --> 00:10:32,597
Sounds like she might have
some first edition Melvilles.

109
00:10:32,765 --> 00:10:35,200
They will still be there
when the test is over, Wally.

110
00:10:35,368 --> 00:10:38,270
Come on, when was the last
time you saw a first-edition Melville?

111
00:10:38,438 --> 00:10:40,532
How long are you gonna be?

112
00:10:40,707 --> 00:10:43,199
Uh, a couple of hours.

113
00:10:44,144 --> 00:10:47,137
Thanks, Tom, I
really appreciate this.

114
00:11:07,066 --> 00:11:08,125
<i>Come here.</i>

115
00:11:26,886 --> 00:11:29,726
McDANlELS: I'll type up the appraisals
and bring them by in the morning.

116
00:11:29,856 --> 00:11:32,485
You're so kind to have come
over on such short notice.

117
00:11:32,659 --> 00:11:35,094
Oh, please, as I've told
you, books are my passion.

118
00:11:35,261 --> 00:11:37,355
Heh, that is abundantly clear.

119
00:11:37,530 --> 00:11:41,092
As Descartes said, "The
reading of all good books

120
00:11:41,267 --> 00:11:45,295
is like conversations with the
finest men of past centuries."

121
00:11:45,471 --> 00:11:47,269
I love that.

122
00:11:48,908 --> 00:11:49,967
Hold it.

123
00:11:50,143 --> 00:11:53,375
Mr. McDaniels, I'm afraid I'm gonna
have to ask you open your briefcase.

124
00:11:53,546 --> 00:11:55,538
- Frank.
- I don't understand.

125
00:11:55,715 --> 00:11:58,275
Sir, your briefcase.

126
00:11:58,451 --> 00:12:00,443
Frank, do you
realize who this is?

127
00:12:00,620 --> 00:12:02,987
Look, I'm sorry, Ms. Reynolds.

128
00:12:03,156 --> 00:12:06,024
But we got a call saying that
Mr. McDaniels stole some books

129
00:12:06,192 --> 00:12:08,627
from the collection the
museum received the other day.

130
00:12:08,795 --> 00:12:10,955
I spoke with the director.
He asked me to check it out.

131
00:12:11,097 --> 00:12:12,537
I'm so embarrassed.
You want me to...

132
00:12:12,632 --> 00:12:16,160
No. That's all right.

133
00:12:26,713 --> 00:12:29,148
Oh, my Lord.

134
00:12:29,315 --> 00:12:31,375
Good work, officer.

135
00:12:31,551 --> 00:12:33,816
It was Tom Petri that
called you, wasn't it?

136
00:12:37,390 --> 00:12:38,858
Son of a gun.

137
00:12:48,868 --> 00:12:51,360
You're...? Uh, you're a thief?

138
00:12:51,537 --> 00:12:55,565
Yes, I guess, technically, I
would be considered a thief.

139
00:12:55,742 --> 00:12:56,766
But my intent...

140
00:12:56,943 --> 00:12:58,935
Uh, Wally, we've
been friends for years.

141
00:12:59,112 --> 00:13:01,479
We've sung in the same
choir for seven years.

142
00:13:01,648 --> 00:13:06,746
Don't tell me that you've been
stealing books for all those seven years.

143
00:13:06,919 --> 00:13:08,888
- No, not for seven years.
- Thank God.

144
00:13:09,055 --> 00:13:12,150
- Fourteen.
- Oh, my...

145
00:13:12,325 --> 00:13:15,955
- I'll never get you off.
- I don't want you to.

146
00:13:16,929 --> 00:13:20,991
- You don't want me to?
- No. Ben, I did this.

147
00:13:22,702 --> 00:13:25,729
I've stolen hundreds of
books for over a decade.

148
00:13:25,905 --> 00:13:29,171
I'm pleading guilty.
Somebody has got to stop me.

149
00:13:29,342 --> 00:13:32,710
The reason I called you was I
hoping you could get me out on bail

150
00:13:32,879 --> 00:13:36,043
so I can straighten out my
affairs before serving my time.

151
00:13:36,215 --> 00:13:38,343
- Can I ask you something?
- Anything.

152
00:13:39,752 --> 00:13:41,448
What in the hell's
wrong with you?

153
00:13:41,621 --> 00:13:45,319
- It's not like it's made you rich.
- Heh. Oh, Lord, no.

154
00:13:48,294 --> 00:13:51,196
- You collect antique cars, right?
- Yeah.

155
00:13:51,364 --> 00:13:54,163
- Why?
- I like them.

156
00:13:54,334 --> 00:13:55,825
I can resell them at a profit.

157
00:13:56,002 --> 00:13:58,494
But you haven't sold them
because you cherish them

158
00:13:58,671 --> 00:14:01,641
and you want other
people to see them.

159
00:14:02,275 --> 00:14:04,005
That's the way I
feel about rare books.

160
00:14:05,211 --> 00:14:07,976
Well, why steal them?
Why not just read them?

161
00:14:08,147 --> 00:14:12,585
Because I want the world to
revel in their brilliance, as I do.

162
00:14:12,752 --> 00:14:15,221
The museums, galleries,

163
00:14:15,388 --> 00:14:17,914
maybe they'll show off a
collection every now and then.

164
00:14:18,558 --> 00:14:22,620
In a library, people can touch
them. They can read them.

165
00:14:22,795 --> 00:14:28,063
So you... You've been stealing books
from galleries to give to public libraries.

166
00:14:28,234 --> 00:14:29,759
Yes.

167
00:14:31,537 --> 00:14:34,473
Wally, you're a weird dude.

168
00:14:37,076 --> 00:14:41,480
Ben, I know what
I did was wrong.

169
00:14:42,181 --> 00:14:44,241
And I know I've
got to be punished.

170
00:14:44,417 --> 00:14:47,910
I tried to stop, believe
me. I just couldn't.

171
00:14:48,654 --> 00:14:52,682
Truth is, Tom Petri actually
did me a favor by turning me in.

172
00:15:44,177 --> 00:15:47,375
<i>Sing hallelujah praise the Lord</i>

173
00:15:47,547 --> 00:15:50,813
<i>Sing with a cheerful voice</i>

174
00:15:51,584 --> 00:15:58,957
<i>Exalt our God with one
accord And in his name rejoice</i>

175
00:15:59,125 --> 00:16:03,529
<i>Ne'er cease to sing
O ransomed host</i>

176
00:16:03,696 --> 00:16:08,225
<i>Praise Father,
Son and Holy Ghost</i>

177
00:16:08,401 --> 00:16:13,066
<i>Until in realms of endless light</i>

178
00:16:13,239 --> 00:16:16,676
<i>Your praises shall unite</i>

179
00:16:17,844 --> 00:16:21,576
<i>Sing hallelujah praise the Lord</i>

180
00:16:21,747 --> 00:16:27,418
<i>Sing with a cheerful voice</i>

181
00:16:30,690 --> 00:16:33,785
Hello, Ben. Sorry to interrupt.

182
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:36,486
What's the matter, Harmon?
Are we singing too loud?

183
00:16:36,662 --> 00:16:39,427
I need to have a few words
with Professor McDaniels.

184
00:16:47,139 --> 00:16:49,267
McDANlELS: What's
this about, lieutenant?

185
00:16:49,442 --> 00:16:52,469
Tom Petri was found dead in his
office at the university this morning.

186
00:16:52,645 --> 00:16:55,615
- He was beaten with a paperweight.
- Oh, my God.

187
00:16:56,415 --> 00:16:58,350
Your fingerprints were on it.

188
00:16:58,518 --> 00:17:00,544
What, so you think I killed Tom?

189
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:03,349
You mind coming downtown,
answer some questions?

190
00:17:03,523 --> 00:17:06,186
Uh, go ahead, Wally.

191
00:17:06,359 --> 00:17:10,023
I'll be there in a half hour.
Don't say anything till I get there.

192
00:17:15,601 --> 00:17:19,766
Well, I guess, uh, rehearsal's over
for today. I'll see you Friday. Thanks.

193
00:17:19,939 --> 00:17:21,805
Sounded good.

194
00:17:29,282 --> 00:17:32,309
I can't believe Tom's
been murdered.

195
00:17:34,287 --> 00:17:37,314
It hasn't been a good
month for Wally, either.

196
00:17:37,490 --> 00:17:39,652
- Excuse me, Mr. Matlock.
- Oh, yeah, Mickey?

197
00:17:39,825 --> 00:17:43,660
Do you think Professor McDaniels is
gonna be out for any length of time?

198
00:17:43,829 --> 00:17:46,890
Because if he is, I could
always, you know, fill in.

199
00:17:47,066 --> 00:17:51,401
- Uh, Mickey...
- Right. Bad timing.

200
00:17:51,571 --> 00:17:54,507
Hey, I took a
shot. It didn't go.

201
00:18:08,621 --> 00:18:10,419
Wally,

202
00:18:11,524 --> 00:18:13,618
if you did this one,
you're out of the choir.

203
00:18:14,293 --> 00:18:18,663
I didn't do this one, Ben. I
didn't even dislike Tom Petri.

204
00:18:18,831 --> 00:18:20,299
I was in his
office all the time.

205
00:18:20,466 --> 00:18:22,146
If my fingerprints are
on that paperweight,

206
00:18:22,301 --> 00:18:24,221
it's because I always
played with it on his desk.

207
00:18:24,370 --> 00:18:26,271
He did turn you in for
stealing those books.

208
00:18:26,439 --> 00:18:27,998
You're gonna serve
time because of him.

209
00:18:29,175 --> 00:18:30,404
I told your father.

210
00:18:30,576 --> 00:18:32,169
He did me... MATLOCK: A favor.

211
00:18:32,345 --> 00:18:33,836
I know.

212
00:18:34,013 --> 00:18:37,677
Wally, nobody's going
to believe you. Nobody.

213
00:18:42,455 --> 00:18:45,254
Is this all Petri had on him?
- Yep.

214
00:18:46,659 --> 00:18:49,060
So you're a choir
conductor, huh, Ben?

215
00:18:49,228 --> 00:18:53,097
Uh, just filling in, Harmon, I'm
kind of assisting the choir director.

216
00:18:53,265 --> 00:18:57,794
- Regular Leonard Birnbaum.
- Stein. Bernstein.

217
00:18:57,970 --> 00:19:02,066
Pop, I don't know whether this
is Tom Petri's handwriting or not,

218
00:19:02,241 --> 00:19:05,143
but someone drew a map to
somewhere, and it's in his wallet.

219
00:19:05,311 --> 00:19:06,677
Hmm.

220
00:19:46,318 --> 00:19:47,752
Yes? MATLOCK: Uh, hello.

221
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:50,082
- I'm Ben Matlock.
- And I'm Leanne Mclntyre.

222
00:19:50,256 --> 00:19:51,349
We're attorneys.

223
00:19:52,391 --> 00:19:53,415
Yes?

224
00:19:53,592 --> 00:19:55,232
I hope we're not
interrupting you.

225
00:19:55,361 --> 00:19:58,729
This may sound a little peculiar,
but we're investigating a murder

226
00:19:58,898 --> 00:20:01,891
and the victim had this map,

227
00:20:02,068 --> 00:20:03,730
which we think he
drew in his wallet.

228
00:20:04,336 --> 00:20:06,737
It's directions to your house.

229
00:20:07,273 --> 00:20:10,368
Did you know a Tom Petri?

230
00:20:11,410 --> 00:20:14,380
No, but these are
directions to my house.

231
00:20:14,547 --> 00:20:18,006
Mr. Petri was a professor
at Baxter University.

232
00:20:18,918 --> 00:20:22,411
